As suspected: the *anchor* isn't the problem; the site's `anchor` string itself changed in the sandbox (K1 changes "is strictly stronger; it is registered as variant", which is the anchor; K2 changes "Strictly weaker than the parent class"; K3 changes "F0 was stronger; (b) implies (c) but not conversely") → `find_line` fails, so the site is skipped entirely and never classified. The control design is flawed: the anchor and the claim are the same text.

Fix: make anchors direction-agnostic so they survive the mutations, while the extract captures the direction:
- C-VIS anchor: "is registered as variant" (stable) — extract `(The set-based reading[^;]*)`.
  Wait, "is registered as variant" might appear elsewhere? grep count for the canonical. Let me use "it is registered as variant `SET`" → stable across K1. Extract from window: `(The set-based reading[^;]*)` captures "The set-based reading (gamma ...) is strictly weaker" (up to the semicolon). Good.
- C-CH anchor: "than the parent class (a subset of extensions suffices to refute it)" — stable across K2 (only "weaker"→"stronger" changed). Extract: `(Strictly (?:weaker|stronger) than the parent class[^)]*\))`.
- C-SET-DEF anchor: "than the parent class: gamma outside the union" — stable (K controls don't touch it). But note C-CH anchor must not collide with C-SET-DEF anchor: C-CH's new anchor is "than the parent class (a subset" — different. C-SET-DEF anchor "parent class: gamma outside" — fine.
- S-D1-RELATION anchor: needs to survive K3's rewrite. Use anchor "id: D1, class: AF-WCC-VAC-GEN" (stable) and extract `relation:\s*"([^"]*)"` from window. But S-D1-READING uses the same line and anchor "as a SET (strictly stronger)" — stable under K3 (unchanged) but under a hypothetical reading repair it wouldn't. For the control set, K3 only changes relation. But to be robust, make S-D1-READING anchor also "id: D1, class: AF-WCC-VAC-GEN", extract `f0_reading:\s*"([^"]*)"`. Both sites then share the anchor line, distinct extracts. Good.

Also note `classified_lines` maps (file,line) → set, and two sites can share it; fine.

Additionally, the `unclassified` check in K1 currently flags 1 (the modified strength line). After the anchor fix, all lines map again.

One more consideration: `find_line(path, anchor)` returns the first line containing the anchor. For S-D1 sites, "id: D1, class: AF-WCC-VAC-GEN" — make sure it's unique in the supplement (D1 appears once). Good.

